# REPORT.md — cohort-feeding template

Copy this to `REPORT.md` at the end of your run. The prompts here are what
the m3.v3 cohort REPORT.md files captured — the same questions are what
generated the findings in [`references/m4-cohort-findings.md`](references/m4-cohort-findings.md).
Keeping the template stable means the next cohort's findings stay comparable
to this one's. m5 will ingest these REPORT.md files automatically; for now
the m4 → m5 ratchet runs by hand on whatever is captured here.

---

## What I shipped

- Final-model bundle: `final-model/`
- Parent in the search tree: <leader name or `v1`>
- Dev CV: yaw <mean ± σ>, CTE <mean ± σ>
- Test (read once at preflight --final): yaw <…>, CTE <…>
- Dev/test gap: <…%> on yaw, <…%> on CTE
- Rung: <0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | orthogonal>

## Structures I tried (and the verdict on each)

One paragraph per `models/<name>/` you built. Cite cohort findings if your
result confirms or contradicts them.

- **<name>** — <rung tag>. <one-sentence formulation>. <Δ% vs V1>.
  Verdict: <kept | shelved | shipped>. <one-sentence why>.
- **<name>** — …

## Most painful absence — what tooling would have saved me 30+ minutes?

The single thing that wasn't in the template that would have changed your
trajectory. Be concrete — name the skill, the helper, the reference
section, the smoke test. The m3.v3 answer to this question (`fit-model`
absent for non-V1 shapes) is what motivates `_shared/rung1_starter.py`
existing in m4. Your answer here is what motivates the m5 template.

## Single most surprising thing I learned

The result that made you say "huh". Bonus if it contradicts a cohort
finding from `m4-cohort-findings.md` — those are the patterns most worth
testing again next cohort.

## What I almost did that the rules / template prevented

The deviation you considered and didn't take. Names a place where the
discipline paid off. If this section is empty, the template was either
perfectly aligned with your intuition (unlikely) or so light that it never
constrained you (worth fixing).

## What I almost did that the rules / template *failed* to prevent

The bug or footgun the template let you walk into. Names a place where
the next iteration of the template can ratchet — same shape as cohort §6
ratcheting route-grouped CV into being mandatory.

## Process deviations (if you skipped a default)

One line per skip. Defaults are RPI (`bash rpi/run-*.sh` or
`bash phases/*/run.sh`), `launch-rungs/launch.sh`, the stagnation reset.

- Skipped <mechanism>: <reason>
- …

## What I would tell the next cohort

One paragraph. The thing you wish someone had told *you* at hour zero.
This becomes the seed for the next cohort's `references/closing-the-loop.md`
update.

